WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.640
It just went crazy.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:06.760
And the whole thing was like
a split second thing.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.880
And I remember sitting there,
lying there thinking

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:12.520
I'm not going to die here.
I'm not dying here. 

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:23.240
My name's Leroy Smith
and I'm here today to do an interview

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:29.320
with the BBC about an incident
what happened over 20 years ago. 

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.280
I'm James Seymour.
I'm a retired police officer,

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:36.400
now a member of police staff
training police drivers.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:44.200
I grew up with my grandma.
My mum got murdered when I was two. 

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:49.680
And I left school from about 13,

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:54.560
started doing burglaries, robberies,

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:58.720
and went to prison, got four months.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:03.400
Came back out, same thing again.
And this cycle just continued. 

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:07.720
I grew up in Kent.
Came from a very religious background

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:12.600
where it literally the Ten
Commandments were taken as they were,

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:14.320
had no telly, no radio.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.680
By the time I got to late teens,

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:19.880
I decided I wanted to move out
and do something. 

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:23.960
So it was either the armed forces
or the police. 

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.240
In the 90s Brixton was -
and still is - a dangerous place.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:39.840
The whole community is violent
and society is violent in general.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:44.000
But I was just at the time
more violent than everybody else.

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:47.320
Because I realised that that's the
way that you get things done. 

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:50.240
There's a lot of deep feelings,
especially at Brixton

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:54.560
from the 1981 riots,
that were still running very deep. 

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:57.120
You could literally feel the tension

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.120
that any slightest thing
and this could go quite nasty. 

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:11.440
I know that I wanted,

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:14.120
myself and my colleague,
wanted to look for some overtime

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:17.920
and the easiest way to do that
would be a simple drugs arrest.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:27.920
We did a textbook stop,
back-to-back, separated them. 

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:30.240
And I said,

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:34.720
I ain't got nothing to hide
or something along them lines…

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.880
And I had things to hide. 

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:41.840
He dropped something on the floor
that I thought was crack cocaine.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:43.280
So I went to push him
against the wall

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:47.000
so I could take hold
of the evidence.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:49.880
It just went crazy.

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:53.720
And the whole thing was like
a split second thing.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:55.080
So it wasn't like,

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:58.640
I planned it.

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:00.600
It just happened.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:08.240
He pulled a handgun
from his waistband,

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:09.680
hit my colleague in the leg.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:14.160
And it's one of those things where
everything's in slow motion.

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:15.320
This isn't really happening.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:17.840
This is just a nightmare.
I'm going to wake up in a minute.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:19.520
You know,
the alarm's going to go off.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.000
But it is reality. And it was just
a surge of adrenaline.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:29.560
When you live like that, you're not
thinking about other people.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:31.280
You think about yourself.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:34.560
It's a self-centred world
that you're living in. 

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:39.160
The gunman then fired a third shot,
which hit me about

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:42.760
a centimetre from the spine,
went across the top of a kidney,

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:46.920
then ripped out the side of my flesh
outside of my body. 

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:50.240
I remember sitting there,
lying there thinking

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:52.880
I'm not going to die here.
I'm not dying here. 

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:58.760
The two officers, PC Simon Carroll
and PC Jim Seymour, are in hospital.

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:01.480
They stopped to question two men
who were on a motorbike

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:04.440
and both officers were fired at
by the pillion passenger. 

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:13.160
I knew Simon was quite bad
by the way he screamed.

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:14.760
So I tried to get to him.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:19.280
But the pain was too bad.
I just couldn't move.

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:41.440
That is a long time
and I just thought to myself

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:42.800
I'm never getting out.

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:46.960
That's what I thought -
I thought I'm never getting out. 

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:05.360
I read it.
Some of it I didn't agree with.

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:10.040
Some of it I found really
interesting. And I thought,

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:14.920
if he is actually wants to make
a difference to people's lives,

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:16.560
I will support him

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:20.920
and let him know that
I agree with what you're doing.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.120
And I hope you have changed
because I always wanted that.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:40.200
This just feels weird.

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:43.080
You haven't come back at all then?

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:48.080
Nah, I always try to avoid
Brixton if I can, to be honest.

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:50.280
I've been back a couple of times,

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:52.600
but it seems a bit strange
being here with you. 

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:57.120
Yeah, man. I didn’t realise how it
would feel so, so heavy on my heart.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:05:59.120
That's the truth.

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:02.280
Now that I'm here,
I'm kind of lost for words -

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:04.120
that's not normally like me.

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:07.920
I'm pleased Leroy's doing it with me.
You know, it's his moment.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.440
Another moment in my life
I won't ever forget. 

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:14.160
If it was me,
I wouldn't forgive someone,

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:19.520
so if it was a situation now
where something was happening,

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:24.160
I would put my life in harm's way
for him if I had to

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:28.520
without hesitation, because of
everything what's happened.

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:30.200
That's the truth. 

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:34.840
I accept some of my colleagues are
not going to support what I'm doing

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:40.440
because, I don't know, that they
might be "old school" and think

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:43.880
well, he shot two police officers -
they should throw the key away. 

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:49.720
But for me,
that's just very narrow-minded. 

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:53.320
Just forgiving Leroy, it's been amazing.

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:57.160
It's made him feel good.
It's made me feel good.

00:06:57.240 --> 00:06:59.680
You know, when I was suffering PTSD,

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:01.480
it wasn't just because
of the shooting

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:05.280
it was due to a number of
horrific incidents that I dealt with.

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:10.080
But now, without doubt, it's one of
the best things I've done in my life.

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:15.560
I shot the man point blank,
a policeman.

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:20.360
I don't know him from nowhere.
Our paths have collided.

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:24.680
I'm a gun man. I'm a bad man.
He's a law man.

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:30.280
And now I can class him
as a friend.

